;;; W16's family, the `int` activator alone — M1e2 S1e2.2.16, register rows
;;; `W16/int-and-string` and `W16/string-then-int`
;;; (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`).
;;;
;;; The stage file's program with `(note edge "1")` deleted. One activator
;;; compiles one plan, so the compile key has no second activator to
;;; collide with, and this file answers the same in either build and under
;;; either key: it is a control, as `activator-text-string-only.ein` is for
;;; the other activator. The pair that keeps both activators, in the two
;;; orders, is `corpus/fuzz_findings/activator-text-key.ein` and its
;;; `-swapped` twin until the commit that fixes W16 promotes it here: a
;;; build with debug assertions aborts on both, and every in-process corpus
;;; sweep is such a build.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The loader holds `1` and `"1"` as two values, an int and a symbol, so
;;; the KB has two `holds` facts. `(note edge 1)` binds `?R` to `edge` and
;;; `?f` to the int 1, so the rule derives `(noted ?a 1)` for each edge
;;; source `?a`: `(noted 1 1)` and `(noted 2 1)`, both over the int, and
;;; nothing over the string. H is empty (`ein solve -H`, 2026-11-04), so R
;;; is `{∅}`: root, with those two.
;;;
;;; ── What the claim can see ─────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A claim compares a state's facts as printed, and the fact printer drops
;;; a string's quotes (P1e2.3's N5), so `(noted 1 1)` and `(noted 1 "1")`
;;; are one line to it: the claim below holds of this file, of its
;;; string-only twin and of the pair, before W16's fix and after it. What
;;; tells the int from the string is a count: `ein saturate`'s table
;;; (`noted 2` here), the summary's `root.facts_by_relation`, and
;;; `rule_semantics.rs`, which compares the facts by value.

(relation edge  Node Node)
(relation holds Node)
(relation noted Node Node)

(rule note (?R ?f)
  :match  (and (?R ?a ?b) (holds ?f))
  :assert (noted ?a ?f))

(edge 1 2) (edge 2 3)
(holds 1)  (holds "1")

(note edge 1)

(solve
  ;; `holds` and `noted` are named, so both are closed: each state lists
  ;; their complete extent, root's facts included.
  :expect (region
            ;; state 1 of 1 — ∅, root itself · minimal · maximal
            (model (holds 1) (holds "1") (noted 1 1) (noted 2 1))))
